\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

\paragraph{Models.}
We probe three instruction-tuned models with comparable parameter counts but distinct architectures: Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct (32 layers, hidden dimension 4096) \citep{grattafiori2024llama}, Qwen3-8B (36 layers, hidden dimension 4096) \citep{yang2025qwen3}, and Gemma-2-9B-IT (42 layers, hidden dimension 3584) \citep{team2024gemma}.

\paragraph{Dataset.}
We use ConflictQA \citep{xie2024adaptive}, a benchmark of context-parametric knowledge conflicts where a provided context contradicts the model's parametric knowledge.
After verifying that each model can correctly answer the parametric-knowledge question without context, we retain 1{,}368 samples for Llama (1{,}096 train / 272 test), 966 for Qwen (773 train / 193 test), and 1{,}590 for Gemma (1{,}274 train / 316 test), split 80/20 with stratification.
The three models were independently verified, yielding only 3.5\% three-way question overlap (23 questions); pairwise overlaps range from 7.2\% (Llama $\cap$ Gemma) to 24.8\% (Gemma $\cap$ Qwen), so the consistent asymmetry pattern across models constitutes largely independent replication.

\paragraph{Translation.}
We translate all English contexts and questions into Chinese using a Qwen3-8B translation pipeline (EN$\to$ZH), with quality validated by COMET (wmt22-comet-da; \citealt{rei2020comet}) back-translation scores.

\paragraph{Probing.}
For each layer $l$, we train an $L_2$-regularized logistic regression probe ($C = 1.0$, scikit-learn default) on the last-token residual stream representation $\mathbf{h}_l$, with binary labels indicating whether the model chose the contextual or parametric answer.
We report balanced accuracy rather than AUROC, as our claims depend on cross-model directional comparisons; AUROC results are reported in Appendix~\ref{app:auroc}.
All CIs are computed via bootstrap resampling with paired differences for asymmetry estimation.
A selectivity control \citep{hewitt2019designing} with permuted labels on matched-condition data ($N{=}131$--$201$) yields near-chance accuracy (49.2--51.0\%) across all models, confirming selectivity gaps of 21--40~pp.
All experiments used a single GPU; the boundary replication (Appendix~\ref{app:boundary}) used an NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000.

\paragraph{Non-conflict baseline.}
To test whether cross-lingual transfer patterns are specific to knowledge conflicts, we run the same probing pipeline on XNLI \citep{conneau2018xnli}, classifying entailment versus contradiction.

\subsection{Within-Language Conflict Detection}
\label{sec:within_lang}

Table~\ref{tab:main_results} reports balanced accuracy for all probe transfer directions.
All three models encode linearly detectable conflict signals in their residual streams.
Llama achieves 77.6\% on EN$\to$EN (peak at layer 28, 88\% depth) and 72.1\% on ZH$\to$ZH.
Qwen reaches 75.0\% on EN$\to$EN (peak at layer 24, 67\% depth) and 72.6\% on ZH$\to$ZH.
Gemma attains 76.2\% on EN$\to$EN (peak at layer 24, 57\% depth) and 71.0\% on ZH$\to$ZH.
All within-language accuracies are significantly above chance (binomial $p < 0.001$).
Figure~\ref{fig:layer_probe} shows the layer-wise probe accuracy curves for all three models.
These results confirm that knowledge conflict signals are linearly separable in the residual stream across all three architectures and both languages.

%% Figure 2: Layer-wise probe accuracy
\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig2_layerwise_probes.pdf}
\caption{Layer-wise probe balanced accuracy for Llama-3.1-8B (left), Qwen3-8B (center), and Gemma-2-9B-IT (right). Solid lines: residual stream probes; dashed lines (Llama and Qwen only): multilayer perceptron (MLP) sublayer probes. Stars mark peak layers. In the Llama panel, the vertical annotation at L13 marks the layer where steering produces significant causal influence (Fisher $p = 0.002$), coinciding with the ZH$\to$ZH probe peak but not the EN$\to$EN peak (L28). In the Gemma panel, L17 and L21 are annotated as steering-negative layers.}
\label{fig:layer_probe}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Cross-Lingual Transfer and Asymmetry}
\label{sec:cross_lingual}

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Causal influence is localized to L13 regardless of detection peak.}
In Llama, causal influence is localized to L13 (41\% depth; Fisher $p = 0.002$), regardless of detection peak location (EN$\to$EN at L28, ZH$\to$ZH at L13), with all late-layer interventions producing no significant change ($p > 0.3$).
An MLP probe reveals a third dissociation: non-linear conflict features peak at L18 (56\% depth), between the linear peak (L28) and the causal node (L13).
L13 coincides with cross-lingual alignment sites \citep{muller2021first, wendler2024llamas}, suggesting mid-layers serve a dual role in alignment and conflict resolution.
Steering failed in Qwen (L14, $n = 131$, $p = 0.785$) and Gemma (L17, $n = 171$, $p = 0.448$) with zero directional flips; Gemma's 88.9\% probe accuracy rules out probe quality as a confound.

\paragraph{Why does steering fail in Qwen and Gemma?}
Three hypotheses merit consideration: (1)~non-linear causal interactions that single-direction perturbation cannot address \citep{elhage2022toy}; (2)~superposition with other features such that perturbing one direction disrupts unrelated computations \citep{templeton2024scaling}; (3)~architectural differences (e.g., Gemma's sandwich normalization) altering perturbation propagation.

\paragraph{Preliminary hypothesis validation.}
Existing analyses address all three hypotheses.
For~(1), MLP probes gain $+9.2$~pp over linear probes on Llama conflict EN$\to$ZH but $-0.5$~pp on XNLI; Qwen shows a similar pattern ($+2.4$ vs.\ $-2.0$~pp), indicating conflict-specific non-linear encoding.
For~(2), conflict and XNLI probe weight vectors are near-orthogonal (cosine similarity 0.02--0.05), yet cross-task language identity transfer is near-perfect (87--100\%), consistent with shared subspace but distinct discriminative directions---a hallmark of superposition.
For~(3), Qwen and Gemma show mid-layer cross-task transfer dips (L14--L24) absent in Llama; L13 falls in Llama's dip-free region.
Definitive validation requires activation patching \citep{meng2022locating}.

\paragraph{Conflict signals encoded in a general cross-lingual space.}
The ZH$\to$EN transfer advantage ($+7.3$, $+7.2$, $+6.6$~pp) matches the XNLI baseline ($+6.0$, $+6.7$, $+7.8$~pp), confirming the asymmetry piggybacks on pre-existing cross-lingual alignment rather than conflict-specific encoding \citep{zhao2026when, kargaran2024mexa, wendler2024llamas}.

\paragraph{Relation to interpretability without actionability.}
\citet{basu2026interpretability} reported steering failure in clinical NLP despite 98.2\% AUROC probe accuracy.
Our mid-layer steering succeeds with a weaker signal, but the key difference is layer selection---\citeauthor{basu2026interpretability} steered at probe-peak layers, as does our L28 (which also fails).
Our cross-lingual setting additionally isolates architecture via three-model comparison.

